Extend imported-paths to type and trait position

`imported-paths` only ever looked at calls. Its own rationale never was scoped
that way -- "a file's use statements are its list of dependencies; a path
written inline is a dependency that never reaches that list" is exactly as true
of `impl std::fmt::Display for Widget`, which leaves a reader scanning the
imports with no idea the file implements Display.

The omission was found from outside. A sibling repository sat at twenty-one
inline qualified paths with a completely green gate, because every one of them
was a type or a trait rather than a call.

Eight tests first, four of them red: a return type, a parameter type, a generic
argument and an implemented trait. Four green from the start and worth having
anyway, because they pin what must keep passing -- `use std::io` followed by
`io::Result` is still fine, an imported type is still fine, an uppercase
qualifier like `<Vec<u8> as Iterator>::Item` is still a type rather than a
route, and a `use` statement is still where paths belong.

The rule body did not need new logic. The segment test that `offending` already
performed became `offending_path`, and `visit_type_path` and `visit_item_impl`
call it. `impl X for Y` needs its own visit because syn carries the trait as a
bare `Path` rather than a `Type`, so a type visitor alone would miss it.

The tool then broke its own rule in fifteen places -- `syn::Attribute` and
`syn::Type` in test_file_parser, `proc_macro2::TokenStream` in
unit_test_finder, `anyhow::Error` in runner_tests, and
`stern4rust::reporting::offence::Offence` written out in full in eight test
helper signatures. All fifteen corrected with the rule's own corrections, which
is the point of emitting them.

748 tests pass. stern4rust reports 0 offences over its own 172 files with all
21 rules applied.

One diagnostic worth recording, because it cost most of the debugging: the
implementation was correct from the first attempt and appeared not to run.
`cargo clean -p stern4rust` had silently cleaned nothing -- the package is
named `cargo-stern4rust` and only the lib target is `stern4rust` -- so every
run used a stale rlib. A wrong `-p` name is a no-op, not an error.

